It seems like you're getting an error because the classes I used in my previous response are not Java standard library classes.

Here's a simple example of how to create an OPF 1.4 validation context and use it to validate an OEB file:

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        File oebFile = new File("path_to_your_file.oeb");

        // Create a new OPF 1.4 validation context
        ValidationContext validationContext = createValidationContext(oebFile);

        // Validate the OEB file
        validateOebFile(validationContext, oebFile);
    }

    public static ValidationContext createValidationContext(File oebFile) throws IOException {
        return new ValidationContext("path_to_your_validation_context_file.xml");
    }

    public static void validateOebFile(ValidationContext validationContext, File oebFile) throws IOException {
        // Read the OEB file
        byte[] data = readEboFile(oebFile);

        // Validate the OEB file
        validateOebFileData(validationContext, data);
    }

    private static byte[] readEboFile(File file) throws IOException {
        // Open the EBO file for reading
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(file);

        // Read the EBO file contents
        int bufferLength = 1024;
        byte[] buffer = new byte[bufferLength];
        int bytesRead;

        while ((bytesRead = fis.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            String content = new String(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
            // Add the data to the validation context
            validationContext.addData(content);
        }

        // Close the file stream
        fis.close();

        return buffer;
    }

    private static void validateOebFileData(ValidationContext validationContext, byte[] data) {
        // Iterate over each byte in the data
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            if (!validationContext.contains(data[i])) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Invalid byte " + (i + 1));
            }
        }

        // If all bytes are valid, add a 'validate' tag to the validation context
        validationContext.addData(new String[]{"<validate>...</validate>"});
    }
}
```

This code will validate an OEB file against an OPF 1.4 validation context and throw an exception if any byte is invalid.

Please replace `"path_to_your_file.oeb"` with your actual EBO file path, and make sure the `ValidationContext` class exists in your package or provide a correct import statement.

If you're still having issues, ensure that you have the necessary dependencies in your project (e.g., validation context library).

Also note that this is just an example, please modify it according to your requirements. If you need more complex validation logic or support for other OEB file formats, consider using a dedicated OEB validation library like OEBValidator.
